  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Ryan Weathers gave them five and two-thirds clean innings. The Yankees didn't waste it.",
    "what_this_means": "Weathers delivered a quiet masterclass: six hits, one run, seven strikeouts across 5.2 innings. That kind of line wins games. The bullpen didn't fight the finish. Chivilli, Blackburn, and Bednar combined for 3.1 scoreless innings, leaving the door wide open. Philadelphia tied it in the first and never threatened again after Bellinger drove in the go-ahead run in the sixth. This was a pitching duel that the Yankees actually won. The offense didn't need to explode. It needed to convert one chance, and it did. Bellinger went 2-for-4 with the RBI. Goldschmidt was 2-for-4. It was balanced, controlled, sufficient. One run across the final three innings sealed it. That margin holds when your starter owns the zone and your bullpen stays attached to the scoreboard.",
    "what_to_watch": "Three straight one-run wins is not a slump. It's becoming the way this team wins now. Narrow roads, clean pitching, no wasted motion. The formula isn't exciting. It works because the pitching keeps coming up clean and the lineup doesn't demand much. Convert one chance. Don't beat yourself. Let the relief arms hold the door. This is fragile math, but it's held. Bellinger's one swing in the sixth mattered because nothing else did. That's not depth. That's dependency. Three consecutive one-run victories. That doesn't last forever.",
